-# The DevTools Times
+# smallpt browser playground
 
-This project is a sample news application built with Astro, designed to serve as a testing ground for new features in Chrome DevTools.
+Kevin Beason’s [smallpt](https://www.kevinbeason.com/smallpt/) C++ path tracer, compiled to WebAssembly with Emscripten. The original MIT source and license are in `renderer/`.
 
+## Run
 
 ```sh
-pnpm install && pnpm run start
+pnpm install
+pnpm dev --host 0.0.0.0
 ```
 
-The application will now be running on `http://localhost:11224`.
+Open `/` or `/index.html`. The scene renders automatically, with progressive updates from a module Web Worker. Randomize changes the wall and sphere colors and starts a fresh render. Quality and resolution controls rerender the current palette. Download saves the canvas as a PNG.
 
-## Tech Stack
+## Build
 
-- **[Astro](https://astro.build/)**: The web framework for building content-driven websites.
-- **[React](https://react.dev/)**: Used for UI components.
-- **[Tailwind CSS](https://tailwindcss.com/)**: For styling the application.
-- **[Astro DB](https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/database/)**: Powers the comments feature, using a libSQL-compatible database.
+```sh
+pnpm build
+```
+
+The compiled `.wasm` and module are checked in under `public/renderer`, so running the website does not require a C++ toolchain. To rebuild them, install Emscripten and run:
+
+```sh
+pnpm build:renderer
+```
+
+`renderer/smallpt.cpp` retains smallpt's ray/sphere intersections, diffuse/specular/refractive materials, Russian roulette, and 2×2 tent sampling. Its browser bridge replaces the command-line image output with a progressive RGBA buffer. The integrator accumulates linear radiance over passes before gamma correction. No rendering service or external image is used. The initial preview is rendered by the same C++ source at 512 samples per pixel; live rendering replaces it after the first pass.
+
+Non-browser verification:
+
+```sh
+node scripts/check-renderer.mjs
+pnpm build
+```
